<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commission on the Truth for El Salvador</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Salvadoran Truth Commission</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Commission on the Truth for El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> body created under the peace accords of 1992 to investigate serious acts of violence during the civil war. Its report of March 1993, From Madness to Hope, attributed the great majority of the cases it examined to the armed forces and allied death squads. Days later the legislature passed a broad amnesty law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commission-on-the-truth-for-el-salvador.jpg" alt="Commission on the Truth for El Salvador" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Salvador-CIA_WFB_Map_(2004).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
